--TEST--
a literal series name follows one grammar, and it is `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*` — `rule:observability/metrics-three-members`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The grammar belongs to the series rather than to the verb that wrote it, so
// all three are asked here and each is refused for a different clause: a
// capital, a separator the grammar has no place for, and a leading `_`. A name
// a request computes is not read at all -- `nvs_types::intrinsics`' module doc
// owns why that asymmetry is the right one for a metric write.
Core\Metrics::increment("HttpRequests");
Core\Metrics::observe("http.request.seconds", 0.5);
Core\Metrics::gauge("_queue_depth", 3.0);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: a series name starts with `a`-`z`, and `HttpRequests` starts with `H`
  --> case.nvs:7:25
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: a series name is `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`, and `http.request.seconds` carries `.`
  --> case.nvs:8:23
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: a series name starts with `a`-`z`, and `_queue_depth` starts with `_`
  --> case.nvs:9:21
